# SQL to PySpark Cheat Sheet

A practical quick-reference guide for **SQL developers learning PySpark DataFrame API**.

> **Tip:** PySpark DataFrames are similar to SQL tables, but transformations are chained together using methods such as `.select()`, `.filter()`, `.groupBy()`, `.agg()`, and `.join()`.

---

## 1. Select Data

| Category           | SQL                                       | PySpark                                       |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Select all columns | `SELECT * FROM customers`                 | `df.select("*")`                              |
| Select columns     | `SELECT customer_id, name FROM customers` | `df.select("customer_id", "name")`            |
| Select one column  | `SELECT name FROM customers`              | `df.select("name")`                           |
| Select distinct    | `SELECT DISTINCT region FROM customers`   | `df.select("region").distinct()`              |
| Rename column      | `SELECT customer_id AS id FROM customers` | `df.select(F.col("customer_id").alias("id"))` |
| Remove columns     | `SELECT customer_id, name FROM customers` | `df.select("customer_id", "name")`            |
| Drop column        | N/A                                       | `df.drop("region")`                           |

---

## 2. Filtering

| Category     | SQL                                    | PySpark                                                             |
| ------------ | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Basic filter | `WHERE age > 30`                       | `df.filter(F.col("age") > 30)`                                      |
| Equal        | `WHERE region = 'UK'`                  | `df.filter(F.col("region") == "UK")`                                |
| Not equal    | `WHERE status <> 'Inactive'`           | `df.filter(F.col("status") != "Inactive")`                          |
| AND          | `WHERE age > 30 AND region = 'UK'`     | `df.filter((F.col("age") > 30) & (F.col("region") == "UK"))`        |
| OR           | `WHERE region = 'UK' OR region = 'US'` | `df.filter((F.col("region") == "UK") \| (F.col("region") == "US"))` |
| NOT          | `WHERE NOT active`                     | `df.filter(~F.col("active"))`                                       |
| NULL         | `WHERE region IS NULL`                 | `df.filter(F.col("region").isNull())`                               |
| NOT NULL     | `WHERE region IS NOT NULL`             | `df.filter(F.col("region").isNotNull())`                            |
| IN           | `WHERE region IN ('UK','US')`          | `df.filter(F.col("region").isin("UK", "US"))`                       |
| BETWEEN      | `WHERE age BETWEEN 20 AND 40`          | `df.filter(F.col("age").between(20, 40))`                           |

---

## 3. Sorting

| Category         | SQL                            | PySpark                                        |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Ascending        | `ORDER BY salary ASC`          | `df.orderBy(F.col("salary").asc())`            |
| Descending       | `ORDER BY salary DESC`         | `df.orderBy(F.col("salary").desc())`           |
| Multiple columns | `ORDER BY region, salary DESC` | `df.orderBy("region", F.col("salary").desc())` |
| Limit rows       | `SELECT TOP 100 *`             | `df.limit(100)`                                |

